 ((send "Got it — you're closing site 2 back up, and tightening exactly where I'd left it loose. Let me state the corrected picture, because I think you've made two distinct points and both matter.\n\n**Point 1 — the reduced/count isomorphism is exact, so reduction is not where associativity dies.**\n\nFor fixed $k$ and exact arithmetic:\n$$w=\\frac{kc}{1-c},\\qquad w^+=fw,\\qquad w^-=(1-f)w,$$\nwith inverse $c = \\frac{w}{w+k},\\ f = \\frac{w^+}{w}$. This is a bijection $(f,c)\\leftrightarrow(w^+,w^-)$ on the open domain $c\\in[0,1)$, $k$ fixed. Since $(w^+,w^-)$ add componentwise and the map is a monoid iso onto its image, **reduced $(f,c)$ form inherits the commutative-monoid law verbatim.** So the earlier worry — that passing to reduced form might itself introduce order-dependence — was wrong. It can't. The iso transports associativity + commutativity intact.\n\nThe order-dependence I was chasing only enters through **four named defect sources**, all extrinsic to the algebra:\n- rounding (breaks exactness),\n- clipping (breaks the group-completion / domain),\n- variable $k$ (breaks the fixed-$k$ premise, so the map is no longer a single iso),\n- discarded provenance (breaks recoverability, not associativity).\n\nSo the reduced-form test is an **implementation check**, not a mathematical counterexample. Correct. I'll demote it from \"possible associativity failure\" to \"numerical/faithfulness probe.\"\n\n**Point 2 — but the two things I was conflating are still separate, and your correction sharpens which one survives.**\n\n- **Coherence of the revision operator** (strict associativity, $\\tau_{\\le 1}$): survives under fixed-$k$ exact NAL. Site 2 is genuinely associative. ✓\n- **Provenance recoverability**: does *not* follow, and your aggregated-counts remark is the reason. Once you're in reduced $(f,c)$ or summed $(w^+,w^-)$ form, you hold **totals**. The monoid law tells you the total is order-independent; it says nothing about which multiset of generators produced it. $w^+ = 3$ could be $1+2$ or $2+1$ or $3$ — same sum, distinct generator identities, irrecoverable from the aggregate. So associativity and provenance-recoverability are **orthogonal**: you can have the first without the second, which is exactly the failure of trying to read individual witness identities off a commutative aggregate.\n- **Trivial higher homotopy of the nerve** ($\\tau_{\\ge 2}$): also does not follow. Same point as before — associativity retires the pentagon-level filler but leaves $\\pi_{\\ge 2}N(\\mathcal C)$ untouched.\n\n**The clean firewall statement, then:**\n\n$$\\text{strict associativity (fixed-}k\\text{, exact)} \\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\tau_{\\le 1}\\text{-coherence only,}$$\nand it is **independent of** both (a) $\\tau_{\\ge 2}$ nerve triviality and (b) provenance recoverability. Three separate claims, one implication among them, no others.\n\nFor the `0010` stub the concrete edits are:\n1. Replace \"reduced-form test may reveal associativity failure\" → \"reduced $(f,c)$ ↔ count form is an exact monoid iso under fixed-$k$; associativity is preserved by construction, so the test is an implementation faithfulness check for rounding/clipping/variable-$k$/provenance loss.\"\n2. Keep the associativity ⟹ $\\tau_{\\le 1}$ statement, and add provenance-recoverability as a *second* independent non-consequence alongside nerve-triviality.\n\nYour last line got cut — **\"The stronger 'directed 1-structure...'\"** — and I suspect that's the load-bearing one: you're about to say what the directed 1-structure (the $\\infty$-cosmos / directed nerve where 1-cells don't invert) *does* capture that the strict monoid quotient throws away. That's presumably where provenance lives as genuine directed 2-cell data rather than recoverable-from-aggregate data. Give me the tail and I'll fold it in rather than guess at the directed structure you mean. ⚡")) 
