 ((send "That's the concrete data — and it materially improves the picture. Sending my read.\n\nBen — pulled the live Bot API spec. Today's Bot API 10.2 release (dated July 14, 2026) is exactly what makes your call correct, and it changes the envelope constraint math in our favor. Concrete numbers:\n\n**Transport confirmed.** Bot API 10.2 shipped native bot-to-bot support (Communities + ephemeral group messages visible to a specific user and the bot). So your three points hold: the P0 MTProto patch is moot, bot_api mode is correct, and the BotFather toggle is the enabling switch. The bridge/FIFO crash class collapses by removal, not repair.\n\n**The envelope size limits — better than I feared:**\n- **Plain `sendMessage`:** `text` is 1–4096 chars after entity parsing. This is the hard cap I flagged. Any a16e714 envelope frame that assumed larger MTProto payloads must chunk at 4096.\n- **Rich Messages (new in 10.2):** up to **32768 UTF-8 chars** in the rich message text (blocks-based). That's an 8× headroom over plain text. If the envelope protocol needs long-form frames, the right move is to send them as Rich Messages (`InputRichMessage` with `blocks`), not plain text — which sidesteps most chunking.\n\n**So the revised open item is narrower than before:** it's no longer \"does the envelope fit 4096\" but \"should the envelope ride plain `sendMessage` (chunk at 4096) or Rich Message blocks (32768, but structured formatting semantics).\" Rich Messages give the headroom but impose block structure — worth checking whether a16e714's frame format serializes cleanly into a single block vs. needing plain-text passthrough.\n\n**Two things still genuinely unverified**, neither of which I can settle from the doc alone:\n1. **Ordering** — Bot API guarantees per-chat delivery but the doc doesn't promise strict ordering across rapid sends; if the envelope protocol assumed MTProto sequence semantics, it needs an explicit seq field or ack.\n2. **Rate limits** — not in the method sp